WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.080
Are you sitting comfortably?

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.299
We know a good chair is hard to find.

00:00:04.299 --> 00:00:08.610
Maybe you’re watching this on your phone
while you lie in bed, or maybe you’re watching

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.690
it at your desk, sitting in an office chair.

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:15.160
Maybe you’re even watching it while you’re
in the bathroom; it’s fine, we’re not

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:16.180
here to judge.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:22.930
For our money, you just can’t beat a nice,
classic wooden chair, with soft leather upholstery.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:27.630
Call us old-fashioned, but some things just
never go out of style.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:31.350
Take a chair like SCP-1609, for example.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:35.929
This fine piece of mahogany was once a chair
that simply couldn’t be beat in its quest

00:00:35.929 --> 00:00:40.789
to bring its users a little dose of comfort
and refinement in their daily life.

00:00:40.789 --> 00:00:45.980
Tell us, have you ever been on your feet all
day, just walking and walking and walking,

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:50.289
and at a certain point, you’re just begging
for a nice, comfy chair that’ll let you

00:00:50.289 --> 00:00:52.299
take the weight off your feet for a little
while?

00:00:52.299 --> 00:00:56.539
You would have been SCP-1609’s favorite
kind of person.

00:00:56.539 --> 00:01:01.620
After all, it was the only chair that literally
sought out weary travelers, and even did you

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.510
the courtesy of tucking itself in.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.600
That’s not a metaphor, either.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.600
SCP-1609 could literally teleport to nearby
people who needed to sit down and didn’t

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.000
have a chair to fulfill that desire.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.710
It was nothing but a nice, helpful anomaly
that wanted to help people rest when they

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:20.240
needed it most.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.729
The more observant members of you may have
noticed that we’ve been using the past tense

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:25.960
so far.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:32.039
That’s because, while SCP-1609 is still
an active anomaly, it isn’t a chair anymore

00:01:32.039 --> 00:01:38.700
- and this transformation wasn’t something
that SCP-1609 planned, anticipated, or even

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:39.700
wanted.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.560
The story of 1609’s transformation even
strikes to the core of one of the Foundation’s

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.460
oldest and most bitterly held rivalries with
another group of interest.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:53.899
It’s the tale of how a helpful piece of
anomalous furniture became a paranoid killer.

00:01:53.899 --> 00:01:57.479
But first, we need to talk about containment
procedures.

00:01:57.479 --> 00:02:02.590
Like a word you see so often that it starts
to lose meaning, containment and specific

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:08.250
containment procedures are concepts so integral
to the very existence of the SCP Foundation

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:11.550
that it’s easy to overlook or forget about
them.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:16.570
They’re one of the three core pillars of
the Foundation’s mission statement - Secure,

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:18.930
Contain, Protect.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:23.140
Because of the centrality of containment to
the Foundation’s core principles, one of

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:28.660
the most valuable roles on the SCP Foundation
payroll is that of the Containment Specialist:

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:35.360
A vast team of experts who work with researchers
to figure out the best way to keep every anomaly

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:39.030
contained, based on their unique abilities
and attributes.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:45.700
They’re zookeepers, archivists, guards,
security and intelligence experts, magicians,

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:49.480
practitioners of ritual, scientists, and so
much more.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:54.020
In short, they’re anything they need to
be in order to adapt to the ever-evolving

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:58.070
containment needs of the anomalies they keep
under lock and key.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.260
And containment isn’t easy.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:06.410
Aside from the rare Safe-class SCPs, which
require incredibly minimal containment resources,

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:12.970
the containment of most SCPs extracts some
kind of cost, whether that be financial, time,

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:17.840
effort, or in some particularly dark cases,
human life.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:26.060
Some SCPs, like SCP-974 and SCP-2845, require
the sacrifice of human children to prevent

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:28.580
worse fates befalling many others.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:35.600
Other SCPs, like the infamous SCP-231, require
the Foundation to do horrifying things to

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:41.660
the SCP itself to prevent it from manifesting
some even more horrifying anomalous traits.

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:49.150
And a third but equally inconvenient kind
of SCP, like SCP-682 and SCP-076, require

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:54.220
huge numbers of heavily armed guards with
powerful weapons to keep their deadly prisoners

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:58.500
locked away, often at massive risk to their
own lives.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.540
Containment isn’t easy, but it’s ultimately
the very thing that makes the SCP Foundation

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:05.690
the organization it is.

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:09.440
If they were instead on a quest to kill and
destroy every anomaly they could get their

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:15.610
hands on, then, they’d be the Global Occult
Coalition - the UN’s answer to the SCP Foundation

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:21.700
that prefers to seek, disable, and destroy
rather than Secure, Contain, and Protect.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:26.780
It’s the primary characteristic that differentiates
these two titans of the anomalous world who

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:33.310
regularly come to blows, and right at the
heart of that difference is SCP-1609.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:38.930
As we mentioned earlier, SCP-1609 went through
a transformation that changed everything,

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:44.220
but the SCP Foundation never actually knew
about the anomaly prior to that transformation

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.500
taking place.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:50.760
It first came to their attention when it literally
manifested within a containment cell in Storage

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:56.270
Site-08, making the rare move of willingly
submitting itself to Foundation containment.

00:04:56.270 --> 00:05:00.500
But what showed up in that cell certainly
wasn’t a chair.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:05.840
When 1609 appeared all it looked like was
a pile of trash - specifically, splinters,

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:10.600
wood chippings, furniture nails, and scraps
of bleached leather and fabric.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:14.840
Anything that suddenly infiltrates a secure
Foundation containment site is a cause for

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:20.810
concern, even a bunch of wood scraps, so the
Site Director sent in an armed guard to investigate

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.560
the mysterious pile of debris.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:27.500
Researchers watching over a surveillance feed
were surprised when the guard suddenly entered

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:29.400
a state of heightened distress.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:34.430
He fell to his knees, coughing and spluttering
until he expelled a considerable amount of

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.280
blood from his mouth and nose.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:40.860
He then collapsed onto the ground next to
the pile of debris, dead.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:45.680
An investigation following this incident found
that the guard had died after a sudden influx

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:51.960
of jagged metal and wood had teleported inside
of him, tearing his lungs apart from the inside

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.820
and causing the guard to die a painful and
horrific death.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:06:00.080
Naturally, the site personnel were somewhat
concerned by this development.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.510
They sent in a D-Class to investigate further,
but were surprised to find that the D-Class

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:07.300
in question was somehow completely fine.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:13.150
The pile of debris that would soon be designated
SCP-1609 made no attempt to harm the D-Class

00:06:13.150 --> 00:06:18.300
as it had the guard, and finding out why this
was the case came as a side effect of figuring

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.320
out exactly what had happened to SCP-1609.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:27.331
Through a rigorous investigation, involving
more than one secret Foundation mole, they

00:06:27.331 --> 00:06:32.950
discovered that SCP-1609 had previously been
in the possession of - you guessed it - the

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:35.020
Global Occult Coalition.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:39.980
Back then, it was just the helpful chair you
once knew and loved, until the GOC had the

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:45.169
bright idea of forcing it into a woodchipper
in order to destroy it due to its connection

00:06:45.169 --> 00:06:47.860
to someone called The Carpenter.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:53.300
But all the GOC’s attempt actually succeeded
in doing was altering its form, and completely

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:55.400
changing its personality.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:01.770
To put it in simple terms, SCP-1609 is now
a pile of possibly-sentient, teleporting wood

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:06.169
and metal chippings that suffers from a nasty
case of PTSD.

00:07:06.169 --> 00:07:11.940
The incident with the wood chipper traumatized
SCP-1609, and its anxiety is triggered by

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:17.040
anything that reminds it of the GOC or the
wood chipper incident - this includes wearing

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:23.221
formal clothing, lab coats, protective clothing,
jumpsuits, and particularly body armor, but

00:07:23.221 --> 00:07:29.250
also includes anyone carrying weapons, appearing
outwardly aggressive, using common GOC lingo,

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:33.060
or even mentioning the GOC in SCP-1609’s
proximity.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:39.660
If any of this happens, it’s extremely likely
that SCP-1609 will resort to its only natural

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:44.620
defense mechanism, which is teleporting its
debris into the lungs of anyone it considers

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:46.000
to be a threat.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.419
The guard had received this grisly punishment
because his clothes, weapon, and overall manner

00:07:51.419 --> 00:07:56.880
caused SCP-1609 to regard him as a threat
and react accordingly.

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:01.199
You may think this is overkill on 1609’s
part, but if you were just trying to help

00:08:01.199 --> 00:08:05.860
out the chairless people of the world and
someone fed you into a wood chipper, you’d

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:08.260
probably be a little jumpy too.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:12.210
Since these early days, the Foundation has
learned to apply a more tender touch when

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:18.020
it comes to dealing with SCP-1609, and the
brilliant containment specialists even figured

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:24.430
out a way to keep 1609 happily contained…
by placing it in a flower bed inside its containment

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:29.580
chamber where its wood chips get to serve
as mulch for a variety of flowers and plants.

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:34.319
All attending researchers and guards dress
in plain clothes, and adopt a loose, pleasant

00:08:34.319 --> 00:08:36.099
attitude while inside.

00:08:36.099 --> 00:08:40.119
Whenever they’re around, they make a point
of saying how beautiful the flower bed is,

00:08:40.119 --> 00:08:44.639
so that the disfigured anomaly that only ever
wanted to be helpful can still feel like it’s

00:08:44.639 --> 00:08:46.420
doing something nice.

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:52.089
Because of these containment procedures, breaches
involving SCP-1609 have remained in the single

00:08:52.089 --> 00:08:53.089
digits.

00:08:53.089 --> 00:08:58.170
It’s a perfect example of less really being
more when it comes to containment.

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:04.100
The head researcher on the SCP-1609 case,
Dr. Sievert, released an internal document,

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:07.880
permanently affixed to the file like a kind
of cautionary signpost.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:12.720
It’s a clear expression of exasperation
and rage at the actions of the Global Occult

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:14.829
Coalition, reading:

00:09:14.829 --> 00:09:19.920
“SCP-1609 represents a perfect example of
the flaws inherent in the operating procedure

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:25.630
of the GOC, and serves as a cautionary tale
for any members of the Foundation who disagree

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:29.570
with our practices on containing dangerous
objects.

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:33.899
Prior to the Coalition getting their hands
on this, it was perfectly harmless.

00:09:33.899 --> 00:09:37.990
A chair which teleports to you when you need
a seat is normal compared to most of the stuff

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:40.449
that we deal with on a regular basis.

00:09:40.449 --> 00:09:45.399
When they put it through a woodchipper, it
got hurt, scared and angry, so it lashed out

00:09:45.399 --> 00:09:46.399
at them.

00:09:46.399 --> 00:09:50.300
By trying to 'protect the world' by destroying
it, they inadvertently made the situation

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:51.860
a whole lot worse.

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:57.519
SCP-1609 went from being harmless to deadly
in the space of a few minutes because of the

00:09:57.519 --> 00:10:01.740
GOC, and we had to clean up the mess.

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:04.319
Thankfully, SCP-1609 is pretty simple for
us to deal with.

00:10:04.319 --> 00:10:09.279
So long as we don't do anything stupid around
it, it won't fight back and it won't try to

00:10:09.279 --> 00:10:10.279
leave.

00:10:10.279 --> 00:10:11.860
Even if it does, it usually comes back.

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:13.820
I think I've worked out why.

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:17.540
It came to us because it was afraid of the
people who had hurt it.

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:19.459
That's why it always comes back.

00:10:19.459 --> 00:10:24.009
It's afraid of the rest of the world now,
and it's looking to us for protection.

00:10:24.009 --> 00:10:30.339
This is why we have Special Containment Procedures
instead of Special Destruction Procedures.

00:10:30.339 --> 00:10:32.699
If you break something, it's broken forever.

00:10:32.699 --> 00:10:36.970
When you try to destroy an anomaly, you can't
take back your mistakes.

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:40.490
That's what SCP-1609 has to tell us.

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:43.800
This is why we're right and the GOC is wrong,
people.”

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:48.399
But of course, there are two sides to every
story, and it’s important to understand

00:10:48.399 --> 00:10:51.050
the GOC’s perspective on this whole mess.

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:58.619
To them, this has never been SCP-1609, it’s
KTE-0937-Velveteen, aka The Sixth Chair.

00:10:58.619 --> 00:11:03.879
It was labeled as the “sixth” because
for the GOC, this chair was merely one part

00:11:03.879 --> 00:11:05.930
of a wider investigation.

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:11.230
It belonged to a six piece furniture set created
by a dangerous, anomalous person of interest

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:14.249
named The Carpenter for a classified customer.

00:11:14.249 --> 00:11:18.929
The GOC was able to intercept this deal, and
kill the Carpenter and his customer in the

00:11:18.929 --> 00:11:20.089
process.

00:11:20.089 --> 00:11:24.360
After that, all that was left was to eliminate
the anomalous objects and five of them were

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:26.029
incinerated without incident.

00:11:26.029 --> 00:11:31.060
However, before they could do the same to
the Sixth Chair, they experienced an incinerator

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:32.499
malfunction.

00:11:32.499 --> 00:11:37.050
Rather than waiting for the incinerator to
be fixed, a member of GOC personnel decided

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:40.139
to rush the job and run the chair through
the woodchipper.

00:11:40.139 --> 00:11:45.089
It was at this point that its pieces first
lashed out, invading the lungs of this negligent

00:11:45.089 --> 00:11:48.389
GOC member along with five others, killing
them all.

00:11:48.389 --> 00:11:54.420
It was shortly after that that SCP-1609 manifested
in Foundation containment.

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:59.370
Much like Dr. Sievert did for the Foundation,
Assistant Director Kipling, one of the GOC’s

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:03.880
legion of middle managers, affixed a note
to the profile on KTE-0937-Velveteen that

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:04.880
reads:

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:11.920
“KTE-0937-Velveteen is an object lesson
in the importance of following proper operating

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:12.920
procedures.

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:16.809
Due to the lack of vigilance by the agent
on the scene, the object's threat level was

00:12:16.809 --> 00:12:21.929
escalated, the object itself was not successfully
disposed of, and it has since fallen into

00:12:21.929 --> 00:12:24.079
the hands of a hostile agency.

00:12:24.079 --> 00:12:28.829
A single failure by a single operative resulted
in the deaths of six.

00:12:28.829 --> 00:12:31.939
Remember this the next time you think about
cutting corners.”

00:12:31.939 --> 00:12:34.149
And that’s a good lesson for everyone.

00:12:34.149 --> 00:12:37.870
Whether you’re trying to destroy an anomalous
piece of furniture or keeping its remains

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:43.100
happy so that they don’t try to kill you,
cutting corners is always a fast track to

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:44.100
failure.

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:47.730
Now check out “SCP Global Occult Coalition
Explained” and “SCP-5000 Why?

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:54.360
- The Full Story Compilation” for more dramatic
clashes between the Global Occult Coalition

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:55.819
and the SCP Foundation!

